{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/jolt/1795042000000-engineer-p1b-stage1.prompt.md'","aggregated_output":"Created-GMT: 2026-11-18 19:02:25 GMT\nCreated-Local: 2026-11-19 02:02:25 ICT (+07)\nCoding-Agent: glm\nSession-ID: zcode-subagent-p1b-stage1\n# Task: P1b-stage1 — basic FFI tier on the Gambit target (memory/loader/ordinary calls/errno + real GC + qualified-row gate flips)\n\nRole: VM Runtime Engineer\nImplementers:\n- Model: glm-5.3-flash | Assigned: 2026-11-19 02:1x:xx +07 | Status: active | Rationale: standing ruling PARTIAL-GO; implementation lane per owner routing (glm implements, codex reviews)\n\n## Governing rulings (READ ALL FOUR FIRST — they are the contract)\n\n1. Astra design: /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/jolt/1794976827310-architect-gambit-ffi-plan.gpt-6-astra.findings.md\n   (§1 feasibility + §2 design: typed generated C shims per call shape, invocation-local errno, native bridge compiled ahead of time, loader policy preserved)\n2. Codex review of P1a: /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/jolt/1795030000000-reviewer-p1a-audit-codex.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md (F6 trampoline constraint, gate-table §3)\n3. Codex confirmation: /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/jolt/1795040000000-reviewer-p1a-confirm-codex.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md (C4 must-not-assert list, C5 brief requirements)\n4. Inventory: /Users/sto/workspace/jolt-ffi-inv/p1b-inv/{ffi-call-sites.md,shim-registry.edn,CONSUMERS.md}\n   (commit 98925c18 on gambit-ffi-inv; Q1-Q6 open questions at the end of\n   /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/jolt/1795020000000-engineer-p1b-inventory.glm-5.3-flash.findings.md)\n\n## Standing verdict you implement within (codex C4, verbatim constraints)\n\nPARTIAL-GO: memory, loading, ordinary calls, errno may land independently WHEN\nQUALIFIED. Blocking and generic foreign-thread rows keep message-carrying\nrefusals. You MUST NOT implement or claim: GC-safe blocking, blocking+errno,\ngeneric foreign-pthread callbacks (incl. a \"serialized foreign-thread\" mode),\narbitrary dynamic closure-backed callables (F6: no arm64/x86_64 trampoline\ngenerator in stock 4.9.8), Linux qualification, AOT/FASL/subprocess parity.\nQualification = target-identified public jolt tests, not standalone fixtures.\n\n## Work dir\n\n~/workspace/jolt-ffi-p1b — CREATE IT: cd ~/workspace/jolt && git worktree add\n~/workspace/jolt-ffi-p1b -b gambit-ffi-p1b main. Commit only your changes on\nthat branch, clear messages. Never push. Do NOT touch the other worktrees\n(jolt-ffi-p1/-inv/-mt) or ~/workspace/jolt itself. Gambit source\n~/workspace/gambit-v4.9.8-src read-only. Gambit binaries: absolute mise paths\n(/Users/sto/.local/share/mise/installs/gambit/4.9.8/bin/gsi, gsc; never bare).\n\n## Stage-1 scope (this round)\n\n1. **Native bridge, ahead-of-time**: a C compilation unit (bridge) built with the\n   mise gsc, carrying the typed C shims for the registered ABI shapes in\n   p1b-inv/shim-registry.edn (44 shapes; unregistered shapes raise deliberately).\n   Invocation-local errno capture per astra §2 (no global/TLS \"last errno\" cell);\n   capture immediately after the target call, before allocation/cleanup.\n2. **Adapter routing (stage-1 rows only)**: in host/gambit/scheme-adapter-runtime.ss\n   (fork SHA a6852ef8 — the inventory's citations are current for THIS SHA, not\n   astra's b389f1a7 numbers): implement sa-load-shared-object + symbol\n   resolution (dlopen through the bridge itself), memory ops\n   (alloc/free/typed access/sizes/bulk copies; copy-in/copy-out preserved),\n   ordinary sa-foreign-procedure for registered shapes,\n   sa-foreign-procedure-native-error (two-value return), real sa-gc-collect\n   (the runtime's real collection, NOT a no-op). Route the compiler paths the\n   inventory lists as stage-1 (backend_scheme ffi.clj ordinary-call sites +\n   the :gambit target-prims entry with the unsafe-op prefix decision).\n   Everything else (blocking, foreign-thread, dynamic callables, scoped-address\n   varargs, aggregates/ftype) keeps or gains documented message-carrying\n   refusals — per the inventory's gap findings Q1 (gambit guards for\n   :ffi-layout and named-binding scoped emission must refuse, not die as\n   unbound-variable).\n3. **Gate flips (gambitcheck)**: ONLY the rows this stage qualifies flip from\n   \"must raise\" to behavioral witnesses; ALL other rows keep their refusal\n   assertions. Add the stage-1 subset of astra's §3 gate family as new Makefile\n   targets: gambitffi (aggregate, fail-closed), gambitffi-abi (scalar widths,\n   pointers, byte copies, return conversion, registered signatures, symbol\n   parity), gambitffi-errno-check (atomic capture incl. the intervening-clobber\n   negative control and post-capture clobber, per the repaired W1 pattern),\n   gambitffi-load-failure-smoke (missing vs unloadable, preserved diagnostics).\n   Every target: assertive verdicts (state-derived, watchdogged, no unconditional\n   prints), fail-closed aggregate. Reuse the P1a-r2 witness patterns\n   (~/workspace/jolt-ffi-p1/p1a/witnesses — read-only reference).\n4. **Public-path proof**: the fork's own kernel/eval gates must stay green\n   (make gambitcheck gambitkernel gambiteval under your new build), and at\n   least one public jolt execution exercising the qualified tier end-to-end\n   (a compiled jolt program through the fork's own build path — if the fork's\n   build CLI is Chez-hosted only, run the equivalent gate path and document\n   precisely what public path is and is not yet Gambit-reachable; do not\n   fake a public-path claim).\n5. **Bootstrap the bridge deterministically**: bridge build integrated so the\n   Gambit gates build it from pinned inputs (record digests); no ambient\n   artifacts.\n\n## Out of scope this round\n\nBlocking/foreign-thread/dynamic-callable qualification (refused rows only),\nP2 consumers (crypto/file/parser), Linux leg (orchestrator runs it later),\nMT experiments, anything in datomworld.\n\n## Deliverables\n\n1. Commits on gambit-ffi-p1b with a final state where: bridge builds, stage-1\n   adapter rows implemented, gambitcheck green WITH flipped qualified rows AND\n   retained refusal rows, new gambitffi* targets green, kernel/eval gates green.\n2. Findings file:\n   /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/jolt/1795042000000-engineer-p1b-stage1.glm-5.3-flash.findings.md\n   (standard header; per-capability table: implemented/qualified-by-which-gate/\n   still-refused; verbatim gate outputs; deviations from astra design with\n   reasons; Q1-Q6 disposition so far; ends DONE).\n3. Final response: Completed-* header + per-capability status line + gate\n   summary line.\n\n## Rules\n\nForeground with explicit timeouts; watchdog every gate; keep tool calls flowing\n(poll during long builds with short Reads). This is a multi-hour implementation\nround — work in coherent commits as you go so partial progress is inspectable.\nIf a stage-1 row turns out unimplementable as specified, STOP that row, keep its\nrefusal, and record why — never fake a qualification.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/jolt/1794976827310-architect-gambit-ffi-plan.gpt-6-astra.findings.md'","aggregated_output":"Created-GMT: 2026-11-18 04:44:48 GMT\nCreated-Local: 2026-11-17 23:44:48 EST (-0500)\nCoding-Agent: codex\nSession-ID: 01a1ecf5-dc51-7210-8e9e-79f452f849ae\n\n\nThe supplied checkout has no `build/tools/jolt`, and its toolchain declaration still has an empty patch list. I’ll look for an existing local clone without bootstrapping or fetching. The native sources also show that `jolt-file` is the POSIX file wrapper; the ANTLR wrapper is a separate component that needs its own P3 scope.\n\nI found a clean sibling jolt clone at the exact pinned SHA, so I can inspect the contracts and gates while keeping that source provenance explicit. Two design constraints are clear: Gambit’s boot excludes the FFI runtime, and jolt constructs some foreign procedures from runtime signatures. A `c-lambda` macro mapping alone would not cover the existing API.\n\nThe critical GC gate needs strengthening: Gambit currently implements `sa-gc-collect` as a no-op, so a quick return from `gc-full!` cannot prove that collection completed during a native call. P1 must demonstrate both real collection and progress by another Scheme thread. I also found direct Chez `foreign-procedure` emission in scoped-symbol and varargs paths that must be routed through the adapter.\n\nI haven’t found Gambit 4.9.8 headers or runtime source locally. I’ll distinguish the feasible errno shim design from the release/claim and foreign-thread entry mechanisms that remain unverified, rather than certify the full tier from memory.\n\nThe plan will gate each FFI capability separately, retaining refusal tests for every unsupported operation. P2 and P3 will require explicit Gambit launchers: the current qualification scripts hardcode the Chez launcher. I’ll also call out two delivery prerequisites: `qualify:jolt-upgrade` is documented but absent from `bb.edn`, and the patch verifier needs final-tree validation before successive patches can safely touch the same files.\n\nCompleted-GMT: 2026-11-18 04:44:48 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-11-17 23:44:48 EST (-0500)\nCoding-Agent: codex\nSession-ID: 01a1ecf5-dc51-7210-8e9e-79f452f849ae\n\nIntended artifact: `collab/jolt/1794976827310-architect-gambit-ffi-plan.gpt-6-astra.findings.md`\n\nThe plan follows. **No file was written:** the task explicitly prohibits modifications, and this session’s filesystem is read-only. No builds, gates, tests, or network requests were run.\n\n**Evidence and scope.** This checkout, at `65155a9dd2b5b165b1c1a10009f0d944dbba842c`, has no `build/tools/jolt`. I inspected the existing sibling `/home/sto/ci/datomworld-j6ev/build/tools/jolt`, whose HEAD is the declared pin `b389f1a7fb553f63a3494d71f8fe3251790bf4d3` and whose working tree is clean.\n\nThe supplied checkout also lacks a Gambit entry in `mise.toml`. I found no local Gambit 4.9.8 headers or runtime source. Consequently, the task’s provisioning and historical gate results are supplied context, not independently verified findings.\n\nSeveral source findings materially affect the plan:\n\n- `TARGET-CONTRACT.md` requires atomic native-error capture and GC-safe blocking independently.\n- `host/gambit/scheme-adapter-runtime.ss:321–459` refuses FFI; its `sa-gc-collect` at line 81 is a no-op.\n- `backend_scheme.clj:297` contains only the Chez primitive-table entry. Gambit currently obtains checked operations through the missing-entry fallback.\n- Scoped symbol calls still emit raw Chez `foreign-procedure` at `backend_scheme.clj:2134`; runtime varargs construction does likewise in `host/chez/java/ffi.ss:1294`.\n- Gambit’s boot omits the FFI runtime. Adapter changes alone cannot enable `jolt.ffi`.\n- ANTLR lives in `native/jolt-parser`, not `native/jolt-file`. The latter is the POSIX wrapper supporting J4a–J4d.\n- `qualify:jolt-upgrade` is described as proposed in design §6.4 and is absent from `bb.edn`.\n\n**1. Feasibility ruling**\n\n**Proceed with a bounded P1 feasibility and adapter slice. Do not yet promise complete Chez-equivalent FFI on Gambit 4.9.8.** Atomic errno capture has a concrete implementation path. Generic GC-safe blocking and foreign-thread callbacks need a runtime-source proof and executable witnesses before their refusal rows change.\n\n| Contract surface | Ruling |\n|---|---|\n| Scalar calls, native memory, `dlopen`/symbol lookup | Feasible through a compiled native bridge exposed with Gambit `c-lambda`, with declarations/helpers supplied through `c-declare`. |\n| Atomic C result plus errno | Feasible through typed C wrappers that save errno immediately after the target returns, before conversion, cleanup, allocation, or runtime re-entry. |\n| Blocking calls allowing other threads’ GC | Plain `c-lambda` is insufficient evidence. Enable only after proving the exact 4.9.8 runtime transition and independent Scheme-thread progress. |\n| Same-thread Scheme callbacks | `c-define` supplies an entry mechanism, but dynamic closure identity, rooting, lifetime, exceptions, and re-entry still require implementation. |\n| Callbacks from an unrelated C thread | Unqualified. Requires verified attachment/claim, Scheme entry, and release/detachment rules for the exact runtime build. |\n| Arbitrary runtime signatures | Not provided merely by expanding to compile-time `c-lambda`. Needs a runtime ABI engine or explicitly bounded compiled signature support. |\n| Native compilation, FASL, subprocess | Separate capabilities; remain refused unless independently implemented and qualified. |\n\nThere is an important evidence limit: **I cannot name a verified 4.9.8 release/claim API or declare these rows impossible on every 4.9.8 configuration from the available source.** Supplying exact function names or signatures here would be speculation. This is an unresolved P1 prerequisite, not an assertion that later Gambit APIs exist in 4.9.8.\n\nThe implementer must inspect the exact provisioned 4.9.8 source and record:\n\n1. Runtime build configuration and threading model.\n2. Exact declarations and implementations of any leave/re-enter or release/claim mechanism.\n3. Whether a released foreign frame permits collection and whether another Scheme thread can actually run.\n4. Rules for a previously unknown pthread entering Scheme.\n5. Root and processor-state obligations across those transitions.\n\nNeither `___ATOMIC` nor a C atomic variable establishes these properties. Object-reference release is also distinct from releasing runtime execution ownership. Do not translate Chez `__collect_safe` to a similarly named Gambit mechanism without proving its semantics.\n\nA single processor blocked inside C cannot run another green thread merely because its arguments are rooted. Conversely, enabling multiple native execution contexts does not establish that a foreign frame participates correctly in collection.\n\n**Fallback ruling:** permit explicitly documented FFI sub-capabilities. If blocking or foreign-thread entry cannot be qualified on the pinned runtime, those constructors continue to raise message-carrying unsupported errors. The basic memory/loader/call tier need not remain entirely disabled. Update `CONTRACT.txt` and `TARGET-CONTRACT.md` to describe this partial mode; do not silently redefine “FFI supported” to mean full support.\n\nA worker pool is **not** a transparent generic fallback. Moving calls changes native-thread identity, TLS, callback context, and potentially library behavior. A separately qualified worker interface for specific libraries is possible, but cannot masquerade as unrestricted `sa-foreign-procedure-blocking`.\n\n**2. Adapter and compiler design**\n\nAdd an explicit `:gambit` entry with `:unsafe-prefix \"\"` to `target-prims`. Keep generated code expressed through the adapter boundary; Gambit C forms belong in target-owned bridge compilation units.\n\nP1 must cover all foreign-call construction paths:\n\n- Ordinary and blocking `sa-foreign-procedure`.\n- `sa-foreign-procedure-native-error`, including combinations with supported conventions.\n- Both callable forms.\n- Runtime signature construction.\n- Scoped-address calls.\n- Varargs specialization.\n\nReplace the raw Chez scoped-address emission and runtime `eval` of `foreign-procedure` with adapter operations. Audit aggregate/ftype emission separately. Unsupported ABI forms must raise deliberately, rather than reach an unbound Chez identifier.\n\nThe contract’s “four syntaxes” wording is now incomplete: native-error capture adds another entry. Inventory actual bindings and call sites rather than relying on that count.\n\n**Compilation boundary.** Gambit’s C FFI forms are compiled facilities. An interpreted REPL must not depend on evaluating new `c-lambda` forms successfully. Build a native bridge ahead of time; Scheme adapter macros expand to calls into that bridge. Loading or linking this bridge does not require implementing jolt’s general AOT capability.\n\nFor the first implementation, use **typed, generated C shims per call shape**, shared across symbols with identical ABI shapes. Each accepts the resolved function address. Signature identity includes argument/result types, calling convention, variadic boundary, and relevant ABI layout.\n\nThis gives auditable errno capture and avoids a hand-written universal C ABI dispatcher. It also imposes an explicit limitation: a finite precompiled registry cannot claim arbitrary runtime-signature support. Unregistered shapes must raise. Generate the initial registry from the native consumer inventory and test fixtures.\n\nA later generic engine may use a pinned dependency such as libffi, but requires separate approval of its dependency and ABI surface. In particular, reading errno after `ffi_call` is insufficient unless its intervening return path is proved errno-preserving. Calling a typed shim that saves errno internally avoids that dependency.\n\n**Native-error wrapper.** The essential sequence is:\n\n```c\nresult_type result = target(arg0, arg1);\nint saved_errno = errno;\n/* Only now: store the pair, clean up, or re-enter Scheme. */\n```\n\nUse invocation-local storage, including for nested/reentrant calls. A global “last errno” cell is forbidden; a single TLS cell also fails nested-call ownership.\n\nReturn the two Scheme values expected by the existing lowering. Preserve the target’s errno value on both success and failure; do not normalize success to zero unless the existing contract explicitly requires it. For blocking calls, save errno before reclaiming runtime ownership. Any cleanup that changes errno occurs afterward.\n\n**Blocking transition.** Once the runtime mechanism is verified, the adapter must perform this sequence:\n\n1. Resolve and retain the symbol/library.\n2. Convert arguments and establish ownership while Scheme execution is active.\n3. Ensure C will access only stable native memory or specifically supported rooted representations.\n4. Leave runtime execution through the verified mechanism.\n5. Invoke the target and capture result/errno.\n6. Re-enter the runtime through its required claim mechanism.\n7. Convert results, release temporary resources, and return.\n\nNo Scheme allocation, Scheme-object access, or callback may occur in the released interval without the corresponding entry protocol. Failure and cancellation paths must preserve ownership and reclaim exactly once.\n\n**Memory.** Keep the consumers’ existing copy-in/copy-out design. `dao.posix.file` already documents that caller arrays are not shared with C.\n\nImplement allocation, free, typed access, sizes, and bulk copies with correct widths and pointer representation. Cover `size_t`, signed/unsigned 64-bit values, floating types, null pointers, and byte offsets.\n\nGambit must not inherit the non-moving-collector exemption for `sa-lock-object`. Rooting prevents reclamation; it does not necessarily prevent relocation. For callback objects, a rooted Scheme handle plus a stable C trampoline may satisfy the required callable lifetime. For operations requiring an actual pinned Scheme address, provide a verified pinning mechanism or refuse that operation.\n\nBulk bytevector copies remain short, non-collect-safe operations with no callbacks or allocations while borrowing an address, as the contract requires.\n\n**Loading and resolution.** Preserve the current loader policy:\n\n- Declared libraries use scoped handles and declared-order symbol resolution.\n- Scoped lookup precedes process-global lookup.\n- Missing files differ from present-but-unloadable files.\n- Preserve the loader’s reason, including missing dependencies.\n- Duplicate-symbol detection compares distinct addresses, not handle counts.\n- Handles remain alive while procedures or callable machinery can reference their code.\n\nExpose `sa-load-shared-object`, process-symbol lookup, entry predicates, and addresses through this implementation. Do not replace scoped lookup with global linking merely because that makes `c-lambda` easier.\n\n**Callbacks.** A `c-define` entry alone does not create one independently owned native function pointer for every runtime Scheme closure. Use qualified trampolines/closures with explicit rooting and release, or document a bounded implementation that refuses exhaustion.\n\nSeparate three entry cases: active Scheme-origin calls, re-entry during a released blocking call, and previously unknown C threads. The last two require verified runtime ownership rules before Scheme executes.\n\nPreserve existing callback exception/error handling; do not allow Scheme escapes or C++ exceptions to unwind across an unsupported C frame. Test nested callbacks and callbacks that allocate.\n\nThese callbacks remain host-local runtime machinery. They must not introduce portable yin.vm callbacks or migratable native handles. Application-visible asynchronous effects still enter the stream boundary. A queued event is not an equivalent substitute for a synchronous C callback requiring a return value.\n\n**3. Gate strategy**\n\nMaintain explicit profiles for native FFI and FFI-disabled execution, including Gambit’s JavaScript target.\n\nSplit G1 assertions by individual capability. In the enabled profile, replace each implemented refusal with a behavioral witness using real fixture symbols. In the disabled profile, preserve its refusal. Unimplemented signatures and sub-capabilities retain negative tests in both relevant profiles.\n\nDo not select expectations by attempting an operation and accepting either success or failure. The qualified profile determines the expected result.\n\nRetain native-compilation, FASL, and subprocess refusal rows. A native bridge does not justify flipping them.\n\nProposed targets:\n\n| Target | Required evidence |\n|---|---|\n| `gambitffi` | Aggregate of the mandatory enabled-profile checks; missing prerequisites fail qualification. |\n| `gambitffi-refusals` | Disabled profile, unsupported shapes, conventions, callback modes, and separate capability refusals. |\n| `gambitffi-abi` | Scalar widths, pointers, byte copies, return conversion, runtime registered signatures, symbol/address parity. |\n| `gambitffi-errno-check` | Existing source audit plus executable atomic-capture tests. |\n| `gambitffi-blocking-gc` | Actual collection and another Scheme thread’s progress while C remains blocked. |\n| `gambitffi-callables` | Same-thread, released-thread, foreign-thread, nested, GC, and lifetime cases for every advertised mode. |\n| `gambitffi-load-failure-smoke` | Missing dependency versus missing candidate, preserved diagnostics, direct and project-native paths. |\n| `gambitffi-duplicate-symbol-smoke` | Two addresses detected; shared dependency counted once; declared shadowing preserved; warning policy unchanged. |\n| `gambitffi-error-kinds-check` | Shared diagnostic registry audit plus emitted Gambit error-kind tests. |\n| `gambitffi-zlib-native-smoke` | Declared fake zlib cannot replace the runtime’s qualified/private zlib bindings. |\n| `gambitffi-static-native-smoke` | Explicitly scoped static-link qualification, described below. |\n\nThe existing `errno-check.sh` is a structural audit. It does not prove the native boundary. Add C fixtures returning distinct results and errno values, concurrent calls with distinct errors, nested callbacks, and deliberate post-capture errno clobbering during conversion/cleanup. Include combined blocking-plus-error capture.\n\nFor blocking, use a C-side handshake: announce entry, remain blocked until another Scheme thread completes a real GC witness, then permit return. Run under an external watchdog so deadlock becomes a recorded failure. Include a deliberately incorrect blocking implementation as a negative control.\n\n**The current no-op `sa-gc-collect` cannot qualify this gate.** Supply a real Gambit collection operation and an observable collection witness. Preserve and strengthen the existing crypto/file timeline probes; elapsed timestamps around a no-op are inadequate evidence.\n\nThe zlib and static-native scripts currently exercise **built jolt applications**. If jolt AOT remains unavailable, a `gsc`-linked fixture can qualify bridge-level linking, but cannot count as the original application-build smoke. Record that distinction. Full `jolt build` parity remains Chez-only until separately delivered; its unsupported path stays tested.\n\nRun enabled-profile gates on macOS arm64 and Linux x86_64. Qualification must require Gambit explicitly—using the existing `JOLT_REQUIRE_GAMBIT` convention—and reject silent skips. Keep existing Gambit boot/kernel/eval and seed-generation checks, plus relevant Chez regression checks.\n\n**4. Delivery mechanics and slice boundaries**\n\nBefore P1 implementation, reconcile provisioning with this checkout: declare Gambit 4.9.8, its source/build configuration, resolved binaries, and any bridge dependencies. Do not rely on ambient `gsi`/`gsc`.\n\nEvery jolt change must land as a declared patch under `patches/jolt/<base-sha>/`, with its `:id`, `:base`, patch digest, file before/after digests, generated outputs, and behavioral gate. Changes to datom.world’s wrappers, launchers, and qualification drivers remain ordinary tracked repository changes accompanying that patch.\n\nTwo bootstrap changes are necessary:\n\n- `smoke!` currently invokes the Chez launcher. Extend qualification to run a target-specific Gambit witness and verify reported target identity.\n- `apply-patches!` determines a live stack by comparing every patch’s `:after` hashes with the final tree. Successive patches changing the same file cannot satisfy that rule. Validate ordered patch transitions during application, then compare the live tree with the final expected digest for each path. Add negative checks for partial application, wrong order, corruption, and an omitted patch.\n\nThe existing remint hook runs Chez’s remint script. Backend changes also require deliberate Gambit seed regeneration and recorded digests; it must not be assumed to happen automatically.\n\nImplement `qualify:jolt-upgrade`, or use the documented equivalent checklist with retained evidence until that command exists. Record both the upstream base and complete patch-stack identity. A patch change is a toolchain change even when the base SHA stays fixed.\n\n**P1 — Adapter FFI, boot integration, and qualification**\n\nSuggested patch: `0001-gambit-ffi-adapter.patch`.\n\nInternally sequence the work:\n\n1. Runtime-source audit and minimal errno/GC/callback witnesses.\n2. Native bridge, explicit target entry, loader and memory operations.\n3. Adapter routing for all construction paths and native FFI boot inclusion.\n4. Target-aware launchers, G1 flips, refusal coverage, and new gates.\n\nAcceptance requires real calls through jolt’s emitted code and interpreted runtime, correct memory and resolution semantics, atomic errno, and the exact published capability matrix. GC-safe blocking and foreign-thread callbacks require their own passing witnesses before being advertised.\n\nKeep AOT, images, subprocesses, unsupported aggregates/varargs, and unqualified callback modes refused.\n\nPrincipal risks are runtime ownership semantics, green-thread starvation, ABI mistakes on arm64, generated-seed drift, and a boot that accidentally imports Chez-only FFI facilities.\n\n**P2 — jolt-crypto and J1b requalification**\n\nSuggested patch: `0002-gambit-crypto-qualification.patch`, containing the jolt-side integration/gates; accompany it with target-aware datom.world qualification drivers.\n\nUse the existing C wrapper and ABI wherever possible. Requalify the actual loaded library, even when its path and digest match the Chez record.\n\nRequired coverage includes:\n\n- Hash/sign known answers and cross-host fixture equality.\n- Canonical path, exact version, digest, version floor, repointed paths, replacements, and missing libraries.\n- Wrong-library-first and Linux preload/interposition behavior.\n- Current layout-4 exports and all twenty audited libcrypto entries, including RAND and SHA-1.\n- Acquisition-failure cleanup, zeroization, and no allocation during cleanse.\n- Real GC during the blocking hash operation.\n\nParameterize `qualify.clj` and probe launchers; their present commands hardcode Chez. Every child must report its target so a successful Chez invocation cannot qualify Gambit.\n\n**P2 remains Chez-only if P1 blocking is unqualified.** Removing `:blocking` from crypto calls is not an acceptable workaround.\n\n**P3 — POSIX file/J4d and ANTLR/J5 qualification**\n\nSuggested patch: `0003-gambit-native-consumers.patch`, with separate file and parser acceptance gates.\n\nThis slice must cover both components to meet the requested corpus bar.\n\nFor `native/jolt-file`, require J4a–J4d behavior: byte-identical frames, short/EINTR/error transfers, poison/recovery semantics, descriptor/native-memory lifetime, collection during blocked I/O, wrapper/layout refusal before mutation, locking, close-on-exec, exclusive mode-0600 key creation, saved-state behavior, and cross-host reopen.\n\nFor `native/jolt-parser`, preserve its C ABI, owned buffers, exception containment, worker isolation, budgets, and dedicated parse-thread stack. Run the complete J5 family: budget boundaries, below-bound JVM packet equality, historical crash inputs, injected exceptions, abort-is-failure control, unavailable/wrong-layout recovery, worker reuse, GC/lifetime/cache checks, and REPL transcript equality.\n\nThe parser already creates a native parse thread and then joins it. That join can still block Gambit’s execution context; the parser’s own pthread does not solve the FFI blocking requirement.\n\nAlso audit `jolt_parser_worker_free`: it waits for destruction on a parse thread, yet its current Scheme binding is not marked blocking. Qualify or correct that binding; do not limit the audit to `jolt_parser_export`.\n\nExternal Babashka probe drivers may launch children while Gambit’s subprocess capability remains unsupported. Any probe that actually invokes subprocess creation inside the target requires that capability or remains explicitly unqualified.\n\n**Corpus acceptance means identical semantic results and refusal behavior, not identical timing.** Preserve platform-specific applicability and mark missing legs PARTIAL. Do not label a partial corpus pass as Gambit parity.\n\n**5. Effort and P1 decision**\n\nThese estimates include implementation, fixtures, platform qualification, patch packaging, and review. The approximately 11,000-line adapter is the reference unit; generated seeds and vendored dependencies are excluded.\n\n| Slice | Relative effort | Indicative engineering time |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| P1 runtime audit and decisive witnesses | 0.10–0.20 adapter units | 3–5 days |\n| P1 bridge, compiler/boot integration, gates, patch tooling | 0.45–0.80 | 3–6 weeks |\n| P2 crypto qualification | 0.10–0.20 | 1–2 weeks |\n| P3 file plus parser qualification | 0.25–0.45 | 2–4 weeks |\n| General runtime signatures, unrestricted callbacks, or AOT beyond the bounded tier | Additional 0.30–0.70 | Separate estimate after P1 |\n\nThese are engineering-effort estimates, not a prediction of generated line count. Runtime changes could exceed the upper range; they require a new decision rather than being absorbed silently into P1.\n\n**GO after P1** requires:\n\n- Exact runtime mechanisms and configuration recorded from 4.9.8 source.\n- Both pinned rows demonstrated on both target platforms.\n- Target-identified public jolt execution, not only standalone C/Scheme fixtures.\n- Qualified callable modes and explicit refusals for every remaining mode.\n- No moving-memory exposure across released calls.\n- Reproducible patch application, seeds, negative gates, and rollback.\n- Existing Gambit and relevant Chez gates preserved.\n\n**Partial GO:** memory, loading, ordinary calls, and errno may land independently when qualified. Retain blocking/callback refusals and describe the result as partial FFI. This does **not** authorize P2/P3 parity claims.\n\n**NO-GO for P2/P3:** collection cannot complete while C is blocked; unrelated C-thread entry cannot be made safe where required; exact runtime ownership rules remain unknown; or the solution depends on silently moving generic calls to another native thread.\n\nIf 4.9.8 cannot meet the necessary runtime transitions, the next decision is an explicit Gambit upgrade or runtime patch through the upgrade gate. The fallback remains honest refusal, with Chez retaining the affected capability.\n\nDONE\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/jolt/1795030000000-reviewer-p1a-audit-codex.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/jolt/1795040000000-reviewer-p1a-confirm-codex.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/jolt/1795030000000-reviewer-p1a-audit-codex.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/jolt/1795040000000-reviewer-p1a-confirm-codex.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md'","aggregated_output":"Created-GMT: 2026-11-18 16:58:56 GMT\nCreated-Local: 2026-11-18 23:58:56 ICT (+07)\nCompleted-GMT: 2026-11-18 16:58:56 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-11-18 23:58:56 ICT (+07)\nCoding-Agent: codex\nSession-ID: 01a1ef93-bee4-7c00-9752-4e034b3efcd1\nCreated-GMT: 2026-11-18 16:53:07 GMT\nCreated-Local: 2026-11-18 23:53:07 ICT (+07)\nRole: Review\n\n**The provisioned build’s NO-GO for P2/P3 is correct, but the audit’s supporting claims require amendment.** W3 has an argument-signature defect that invalidates its crash attribution. W1 allocates before saving errno, violating the sequence it claims to prove. A multithreaded rebuild changes scheduling and processor-state access, but the inspected source does not establish GC-safe blocking or documented foreign-pthread attachment.\n\nReviewed jolt commit `dc6176dcc019772c879864a60906217f646b0b3e` and Gambit source `2228e4d163582733a3a67a5efcb22f8ebe93c02b`. Tracked P1a files and Gambit sources remain unchanged. No commit, push, runtime rebuild, or findings-file write was performed.\n\nReferences below use:\n\n- `S/`: `/Users/sto/workspace/gambit-v4.9.8-src/`\n- `H`: `/Users/sto/.local/share/mise/installs/gambit/4.9.8/include/gambit.h`\n- `W/`: `/Users/sto/workspace/jolt-ffi-p1/p1a/witnesses/`\n- `Plan`: the supplied astra design findings.\n\n**Q1 — Reproduction**\n\nI ran `make clean && make all` using byte-identical copies of every witness input inside `W/build/`. This relocation was necessary: the supplied Makefile writes executables beside the sources and deletes/replaces tracked `results/` (`W/Makefile:26–41,45–101`), contrary to the review’s build-directory-only write allowance.\n\nThe make invocation completed successfully. Its process status is a recording result, not capability qualification.\n\n| Recorded result | Reviewer rerun |\n|---|---|\n| `w0.out` | Exact match: single-threaded VMs, maximum/current processors 1, resize remains 1. |\n| `w1.out` | Exact match: 4000 checks, zero failures, printed PASS. Evidence defect discussed below. |\n| `w2-control.out` | Same behavior: real GC reports and printed PASS. |\n| `w2-blocked.out` | Same behavior: approximately six-second block; all B progress and subsequent GC reports follow C’s return; printed FAIL. |\n| `w3-phase1.out` | SIGSEGV, exit 139. Additional repetitions: 3/3 exit 139. |\n| `w3-phase1-nobeat.out` | SIGSEGV, exit 139. Additional repetitions: 3/3 exit 139. |\n| `w3-phase2-run1.out` | Exact match: exit 70. |\n| `w3-phase2-run2.out` | **Mismatch: recorded exit 70; reviewer rerun SIGSEGV, exit 139.** |\n| `w3-phase3.out` | SIGSEGV, exit 139. Additional repetitions: 3/3 exit 139. |\n| `w4-mode1.out` | Same results: M1=40, M2=1107, M3 B result=90 and probe=0. |\n| `w4-mode4-run1/2/3.out` | All three exact matches: callback result 1005; exit 0. |\n| `w4-mode5.out` | Exact match: deliberate exception, exit 70. |\n\nExcept for `w3-phase2-run2.out`, differences were timestamps, GC statistics, or crash-report PIDs. The phase2 discrepancy agrees with the audit’s nondeterminism caveat; it does not change qualification.\n\nReviewer logs are under `W/build/results/`. Generated W3 C was retained there through a separate `make -B w3.exe` using `gsc -keep-temp`.\n\n**Q2 — Evidence audit and findings**\n\n**F1 — P1: W3 exports one argument to Scheme procedures requiring two; its crashes do not isolate foreign-thread entry.**\n\nBoth definitions specify `(int)` despite requiring Scheme formals `(x y)`:\n\n- [w3-server.scm:29](/Users/sto/workspace/jolt-ffi-p1/p1a/witnesses/w3-server.scm:29)\n- `W/w3-server.scm:35`\n\nThe C fixture declares two-argument functions and invokes them accordingly (`W/w3-fthread.c:34–35,60–65`).\n\nRetained generated C confirms the mismatch:\n\n- `W/build/w3-server.c:197–206`: `scheme_w3_add(int ___arg1)`, `___NARGS 1`.\n- `W/build/w3-server.c:222–231`: the same one-argument entry for `scheme_w3_add_alloc`.\n- Generated Scheme procedure descriptors require two arguments (`:1515–1520`).\n\nThe manual explicitly says the C argument types determine the arguments converted and passed to the Scheme procedure (`S/doc/gambit.txi:19399–19412`).\n\nConsequently, the 3/3 crashes reproduce a malformed entry fixture. They cannot prove the audit’s specific explanation of foreign-thread failure, or its claimed W3/W4 frame-state discriminator (`p1a/AUDIT.md:287–312,355–368,483–490`).\n\nRequired correction: use `(int int)`, verify matching generated C declarations, and establish a successful owner-thread control before comparing serialized and concurrent foreign-thread entry. I did not alter sources or execute a corrected variant.\n\n**F2 — P1: W1 violates capture-before-allocation and therefore does not qualify atomic errno capture.**\n\nThe shims read errno after `malloc`:\n\n- [w1-fixture.c:35](/Users/sto/workspace/jolt-ffi-p1/p1a/witnesses/w1-fixture.c:35): target → errno assignment → `malloc` → errno read (`:35–40`).\n- Shape B: target → `malloc` → errno read (`:51–55`).\n- Shape C: target → errno assignment → `malloc` → errno read (`:65–70`).\n- Shape E: errno assignment → `malloc` → errno read (`:87–90`).\n\nShapes A and C also manufacture the expected errno after their “target” returns, rather than preserving the target’s errno.\n\nThis contradicts Plan §1’s requirement to save errno “before conversion, cleanup, allocation, or runtime re-entry” and the immediate-save sequence in §2. Post-capture clobbers cannot detect interference before capture.\n\nThe observed 4000/4000 is reproducible. The stronger claims at `p1a/AUDIT.md:440–462,528–531` are not established. Save `int saved_errno = errno` immediately after the actual target invocation, before allocation; add an intervening-clobber negative control.\n\n**F3 — P2: W2 lacks the advertised runnable contender and completion handshake; its verdict is unconditional.**\n\n`W/w2-blocking.scm:95` says B is runnable before A enters C. Actual execution starts A, sleeps on the owner thread, and only then starts B (`:98–102`). On this build that owner thread cannot wake until C returns, so B has not been started during the blocked interval.\n\nThe C handshake release exists (`W/w2-fixture.c:49–54`) but Scheme never calls it. The blocked mode always prints FAIL (`W/w2-blocking.scm:106`). The control always prints PASS (`:91`); in both recorded and reviewer outputs, B’s GC rounds finish before A’s first nonblocking call.\n\nW2 still demonstrates starvation of the owner’s scheduled continuation, and its post-return collections are real. Combined with the single-processor source evidence, the provisioned blocking refusal remains justified. It is not a reusable success/failure discriminator for another configuration.\n\nFuture qualification must start and synchronize both participants before blocking, release C only after B completes GC, and derive the verdict from observed state.\n\n**F4 — P2: The all-configuration conclusion needs the multithreaded GC and scheduler analysis given in Q3.**\n\nThe audit correctly identifies the provisioned configuration, but extends that into categorical claims about all 4.9.8 implementations (`p1a/AUDIT.md:134–165,613–616`). A search for guessed leave/claim names and single-threaded witnesses is insufficient for that extension.\n\nThe source contains materially different TLS, processor startup, SMP scheduling, and cross-processor return paths. These do not themselves satisfy the contract, but they must be considered before declaring the build question closed.\n\n**F5 — P3: Several doc-first assertions and precise citations do not hold as written.**\n\nSpot-checks that hold include the installed threading configuration (`H:1469–1491`), C-interface nesting warning (`S/doc/gambit.txi:19983–19992`), GC verbosity (`:2230–2237`), `___garbage_collect` declaration (`H:14772–14775`), and `(##gc)` implementation (`S/lib/_kernel.scm:2051–2069`).\n\nCorrections:\n\n- `p1a/AUDIT.md:47–49` says the Threads chapter mentions no OS threads/processors. Its opening explicitly discusses both and says the runtime does not exploit multiprocessors (`S/doc/gambit.txi:9843–9847`). That manual text also omits the source’s optional SMP variant.\n- The conversion-temporary guarantee cited at manual `19296–19300` is actually at `19305–19307` (`p1a/AUDIT.md:414–419`).\n- The `___AT_END` guarantee cited at `19313–19319` is actually at `19328–19332` (`p1a/AUDIT.md:419–420`).\n- The stack-marker rooting TODO cited at `S/lib/c_intf.c:6528–6529` is at `:6530`.\n- The resize clamp cited at `S/lib/setup.c:1163–1166` is at `:1169–1170`. GC’s `on_all_processors` call is at `:1196`, outside the cited `1181–1194` interval.\n\nHeader declarations are useful evidence of available functions; they do not, by themselves, document a foreign-thread lifecycle protocol.\n\n**Q3 — Adjudication of a differently configured 4.9.8 build**\n\n**Configuration is consequential, but changing configuration alone does not establish the required capabilities.**\n\nFirst, `--enable-single-host` is code generation, not the cause of single-threaded VMs. The mise plugin passes that option and omits the threading options (`~/.local/share/mise/plugins/gambit/lib/utils.bash:68`), leaving their defaults in effect.\n\nThe relevant 4.9.8 options are:\n\n- `--enable-multiple-threaded-vms`: enables multiple OS threads per VM (`S/configure.ac:1267–1283`).\n- `--enable-smp`: independently selects the SMP Scheme scheduler (`:1287–1299`).\n- `--enable-max-processors=N`: raises the maximum only with multiple-threaded VMs (`:1303–1316`).\n\nI found no `--enable-multiprocessor` option in this source.\n\n**(a) Release/claim around foreign calls: no qualifying mechanism established in either configuration.**\n\nThe FFI macros save and restore Scheme registers around C execution (`H:10310–10334`); they do not mark that processor absent from GC participation or transfer execution ownership.\n\nThe multithreaded branch changes processor lookup from the fixed primordial state to TLS:\n\n- Single-threaded: `S/include/gambit.h.in:8607–8612`.\n- Multithreaded: TLS getter/setter or TLS variable at `:8673–8685`.\n\nTLS selection supplies a processor-state address. It is not a release/claim protocol. Neither the manual nor the inspected alternate FFI paths supply the required leave/re-enter lifecycle.\n\n**(b) GC completing while a normal foreign call remains blocked: the multithreaded source retains a decisive obstacle.**\n\nThe synchronization path is:\n\n1. `___garbage_collect` requests `OP_GARBAGE_COLLECT` through `on_all_processors` (`S/lib/setup.c:1182–1196`).\n2. `on_all_processors` interrupts the VM’s processors and enters the synchronization loop (`:1132–1149`).\n3. The multithreaded barrier includes `vms->processor_count` participants and waits for their messages (`:500–515,554–580`).\n4. Collection executes after barrier selection (`:1065–1068,1098–1099,1028–1029`); the collector itself also uses barriers (`S/lib/mem.c:7002`).\n\nA processor stuck in an ordinary synchronous C call does not execute `service_sync_op`. The FFI macros do not exempt it. Thus additional processors may permit Scheme progress before a collection request, but do not establish collection completion while that C call remains blocked.\n\nThis is a source-based conclusion, not an executed multithreaded result. Any proposed bridge that changes this behavior must identify the exact transition and root-update rules it uses.\n\nThe separate SMP scheduler matters: `S/lib/_thread.scm:9–24` selects its implementation; `:3110–3125` starts scheduling on additional processors. The non-SMP additional-processor startup instead waits for interrupts (`:6866–6889`). Multiple-threaded VMs alone must not be equated with parallel scheduling of the witness’s ordinary Scheme threads.\n\n**(c) Documented foreign-pthread entry: no.**\n\nThe manual’s server pattern requires `___setup` before calls (`S/doc/gambit.txi:19440–19452`), but provides no unrelated-pthread attach/claim/detach procedure.\n\nThe alternate runtime does contain ingredients:\n\n- Runtime-created processors bind their OS thread to a pstate (`S/lib/setup.c:805–835`).\n- Binding uses `___thread_set_pstate` (`S/lib/os_thread.c:697–708`).\n- Processor creation includes state initialization, synchronized membership changes, and OS-thread creation (`S/lib/setup.c:940–964,987–996`).\n\nThese operations are not a documented recipe for attaching an arbitrary caller. An unregistered pthread does not acquire a valid processor merely by calling `c-define`. The multithreaded TLS path changes the failure mechanism from shared-state reuse; it does not automatically make entry valid. The cross-processor C-return guard (`S/lib/_kernel.c:11743–11755`) likewise protects return routing rather than attaching new threads.\n\n**Version property or configuration property?**\n\nThe one-processor starvation is a **configuration property**. The missing documented attachment protocol and the inspected synchronous FFI/all-processor-GC behavior are limitations of the **stock 4.9.8 source**, including its multithreaded variant.\n\nAccordingly, neither “W2/W3 prove every 4.9.8 configuration impossible” nor “enable SMP and the contract is satisfied” is warranted. Configuration-only adoption does not clear the gate.\n\n**Experiment needed to challenge this conclusion**\n\nUse a separate, pinned 4.9.8 build with `--enable-multiple-threaded-vms --enable-smp`, at least two processors, and explicit runtime parallelism such as `-:p2` (`S/lib/main.c:46–49`). Record compiler, configure arguments, header, library, and processor identities.\n\nThen:\n\n1. Use valid processor startup. Do not blindly reuse W0’s `(##current-vm-resize #f 4)`: new processors execute the supplied thunk (`S/lib/setup.c:810–835,992–994`). Use normal startup parallelism or the runtime’s scheduler startup path.\n2. Repair W2’s synchronization. Confirm A is inside C and B is executing on a different processor; require B to finish a real collection and update live rooted objects **before** it signals C to return. Detect timeout independently.\n3. Run both ordinary `c-lambda` and any specifically proposed release/claim bridge. Cite the latter’s exact implementation.\n4. Repair W3’s signatures and establish the owner-thread control. Test serialized and concurrent foreign entry separately, then allocation/GC, nesting, cleanup, and repeated attachment. Any proposed attachment sequence must explain membership, ownership, TLS, catcher, roots, and teardown.\n5. Run on macOS arm64 and Linux x86_64. A changed crash signature or extra Scheme progress alone is not a pass.\n\nNo multithreaded rebuild was executed in this review.\n\n**Q4 — Verdict sanity against astra’s criteria**\n\nThe engineer’s **NO-GO for P2/P3 on the provisioned build is correct**. Blocking remains unqualified, and foreign-thread entry lacks a verified protocol. However, withdraw “W3 proves unrelated entry cannot be made safe”: F1 invalidates that experimental inference.\n\nPlan §5 states verbatim:\n\n> **Partial GO:** memory, loading, ordinary calls, and errno may land independently when qualified. Retain blocking/callback refusals and describe the result as partial FFI. This does **not** authorize P2/P3 parity claims.\n\nW1’s qualification claim must be repaired under F2. Memory and loading were not qualified by P1a. This is authorization to pursue a bounded P1b, not acceptance of the complete basic tier.\n\nPlan §5’s NO-GO criteria are:\n\n> **NO-GO for P2/P3:** collection cannot complete while C is blocked; unrelated C-thread entry cannot be made safe where required; exact runtime ownership rules remain unknown; or the solution depends on silently moving generic calls to another native thread.\n\n**Adopting multithreaded 4.9.8 does not change the track to GO.** P2/P3 remain NO-GO pending exact mechanisms and passing qualification. That adoption reopens configuration-specific P1 evidence; current single-threaded observations cannot qualify it.\n\nThe upgrade language remains applicable verbatim:\n\n> If 4.9.8 cannot meet the necessary runtime transitions, the next decision is an explicit Gambit upgrade or runtime patch through the upgrade gate. The fallback remains honest refusal, with Chez retaining the affected capability.\n\nAn alternate configuration is not itself a runtime patch. It nevertheless requires explicit provisioning and qualification. If the proposed solution changes runtime transitions, it falls under that upgrade/patch decision.\n\nFull GO additionally requires both platforms, target-identified public jolt execution, qualified callable modes/refusals, memory safety, patch/seed reproducibility, rollback, and preserved regression gates (`Plan:267–275`).\n\n**Q5 — Additional material implications for P1b**\n\n**F6 — P2: Static callbacks do not qualify dynamic closure-backed callables; the built-in dynamic trampoline generator lacks both target architectures.**\n\nThe manual permits procedure-to-function conversion (`S/doc/gambit.txi:19098–19101`). Its implementation distinguishes static `c-define` representatives from other procedures:\n\n- Static representative lookup: `S/lib/c_intf.c:2914–2923`.\n- Other procedures require `___make_c_closure`; failure becomes a conversion error (`:2925–2933`).\n- `C_CLOSURE_CODE_SIZE` is supplied only for 32-bit x86, selected PowerPC, and SPARC cases (`S/lib/os_dyn.c:639–670`).\n- Without it, `___make_c_closure` returns null (`:758–760`).\n\nThere is no corresponding arm64 or x86_64 generator here. Furthermore, the existing generator’s closure identity storage is global with a TODO to make it thread-local (`:744–745`).\n\nW4’s static `c-define` functions therefore do not qualify arbitrary runtime closures. P1b needs independently qualified stable trampolines and rooted closure identity/lifetime, or explicit refusals.\n\nOther decision-relevant limits:\n\n- **Qualification must assert behavior.** W2/W4 print some verdicts unconditionally; Makefile recording recipes consume child statuses and continue (`W/Makefile:45–95`). Preserve these as diagnostic recorders, but make actual qualification reject wrong values, absent handshakes, timeouts, and unexpected exits.\n- **Same-thread callbacks still have scheduling restrictions.** The manual warns about switching between nested Scheme→C→Scheme chains (`S/doc/gambit.txi:19983–19992`). W4’s simple nesting does not qualify callbacks that yield, block, or migrate.\n- **Exception containment remains necessary.** W4-M5 reproduces fatal exit 70; the server example catches exceptions inside the callable (`S/tests/server.scm:13–17,32–34`).\n- **Real collection remains separate work.** Gambit’s adapter still returns `#f` from `sa-gc-collect` (`host/gambit/scheme-adapter-runtime.ss:81–82`). Runtime `(##gc)` exists, but bridge-level reports do not qualify public jolt GC behavior.\n- The inventory lane’s compiler refusal gaps, omitted callable contract entries, parser destruction wait, and unmarked SSL waits remain P1b follow-ups. A runtime rebuild does not resolve those construction and capability-boundary questions.\n\nVERDICT-AMENDED — NO-GO for P2/P3 on the provisioned build; a multithreaded 4.9.8 configuration remains unqualified and does not clear that verdict. PARTIAL-GO permits bounded P1b work only as each capability is independently qualified; W1 atomicity and W3 foreign-entry claims require correction.\nCreated-GMT: 2026-11-18 18:59:06 GMT\nCreated-Local: 2026-11-19 01:59:06 ICT (+07)\nCompleted-Local: 2026-11-19 01:59:06 ICT (+07)\nCoding-Agent: codex\nSession-ID: 01a1ef93-bee4-7c00-9752-4e034b3efcd1\nRole: Review\n\n**The bounded P1b ruling is confirmed. F1–F3 are repaired for the provisioned-build audit. The MT blocked-GC failure also reproduces. However, the MT report overstates its teardown evidence, and its “183µs after return” timing claim needs correction.**\n\nReviewed commits:\n\n- P1a-r2: `6507ec1ed5533504b22955aa20a37af9e1d1e155`.\n- P1a-MT: `1b9b6ed09f966dfe2e5e065938be67ca16f17477`.\n- Gambit: `2228e4d163582733a3a67a5efcb22f8ebe93c02b`.\n\nI ran the r2 suite and generated-C verification, repeated repaired W3 rows, and reran existing MT configuration, blocked-GC, owner-control, and naive serialized-entry binaries. Writes were confined to `p1a/witnesses/build/`. Both worktrees’ tracked files remain unchanged.\n\nPath abbreviations: `W/` = this worktree’s `p1a/witnesses/`; `MT/` = `/Users/sto/workspace/jolt-ffi-mt/p1a-mt/`; `S/` = `/Users/sto/workspace/gambit-v4.9.8-src/`.\n\n**C1 — Repairs against F1–F3**\n\n**F1 repair — CONFIRMED.**\n\nThe two add entries now specify `(int int)`; the zero-argument phase3 entry specifies `()` (`W/w3-server.scm:56–63,96–97`). Generated C contains two C arguments and `___NARGS 2` for both add entries (`W/build/w3-server.c:197–205,228–233`). `make verify-w3-c` passed, including its phase3 `___NARGS 0` check (`W/Makefile:88–100`).\n\nEvery provisioned-build phase runs the owner control first and aborts on control failure (`W/w3-fthread.c:74–101,267–275`).\n\nMy samples reproduced:\n\n| Repaired W3 row | Reviewer result |\n|---|---|\n| Owner control | PASS: 42, 1072, two GC rounds |\n| Serialized phase1 | 3/3 survived with correct recorded values |\n| Serialized phase1, heartbeat disabled | 3/3 survived |\n| Owner parked inside cfun, phase3 | 3/3 survived |\n| Concurrent phase2 | 3/3 SIGSEGV, exit 139 |\n\nThe serialized workers reached the three `(##gc)` rounds and final result 2 (`W/w3-fthread.c:125–131`; `W/results/w3-phase3.out:10–20`). The original malformed-fixture crash attribution must remain withdrawn.\n\n**F2 repair — CONFIRMED.**\n\nThe actual targets now set their own failure errno (`W/w1-fixture.c:53–55`). Shapes A/B/C save errno immediately after the target and before allocation (`:64–68,81–85,97–101`). Shape E saves before allocation and nested Scheme re-entry (`:120–125`).\n\nThe contrasting controls are effective:\n\n- F saves before the intervening clobber (`:144–149`).\n- G deliberately saves after it (`:163–168`).\n- Scheme checks G’s clobbered result explicitly and exercises F/G in the loops (`W/w1-errno.scm:138–163`).\n\nMy rerun produced **5901 checks, zero failures, negative-control OK** (`W/build/results/w1.out:4–8`). This confirms the repaired standalone capture witness; it does not yet qualify jolt’s future shim generator or blocking-plus-errno path.\n\n**F3 repair — CONFIRMED for the provisioned-build refusal witness.**\n\nB executes and announces readiness before A enters C (`W/w2-blocking.scm:199–217`). B mutates a globally rooted object, performs real GC, and calls the C handshake only after a completed collection (`:111–145`). C records release cause and whether the handshake arrived during its active wait (`W/w2-fixture.c:47–83,91–104`). The qualification verdict derives from observed state (`W/w2-blocking.scm:223–263`).\n\nMy rerun reproduced:\n\n```text\nrelease-cause=deadline\nrounds-during-block=0\nrounds-after=3\nhandshake-called-from-scheme=#t\ncapi-handshake-calls=1\nhandshake-during-block=0\nblocked-ms=6000\n```\n\nEvidence: `W/build/results/w2-qualify.out:22–25`.\n\nOne limitation remains: the control’s “overlap” predicate permits all B collections before A’s first call because it tests only `a-calls-done < 3` (`W/w2-blocking.scm:128–129,182–186`). Treat it as a working-GC control, not proof of collection overlapping C execution. This does not undermine the synchronized negative qualification result.\n\n`make all` and `make verify-w3-c` passed. Build hygiene now preserves tracked results and existing `build/results/` through `clean` (`W/Makefile:239–258`). `VERIFY: PASS` means the expected audit records—including capability failures—were obtained.\n\nThe F5 citation corrections are also present, including the Threads introduction, conversion-temporary and `___AT_END` passages, stack-marker TODO, and corrected synchronization locations (`p1a/AUDIT.md:78–83,115–120,588–599`).\n\n**C2 — Effect of serialized foreign-entry survival**\n\nIt narrows the empirical conclusion materially:\n\n**On this provisioned single-threaded build, a foreign pthread can execute these valid entries, allocate, collect, and return while the owner is parked.** A categorical assertion that foreign entry necessarily crashes is false.\n\nThe qualification boundary remains unchanged. These samples do not establish a documented ownership-transfer protocol, exclusion against every runtime activity, lifecycle safety, nested-entry rules, or portability. Concurrent use of the shared pstate still crashes. The configured single-threaded processor accessor selects the same primordial pstate regardless of calling pthread (`S/include/gambit.h.in:8607–8612`).\n\nP1b may qualify callbacks on the owning native thread under explicit nesting, scheduling, lifetime, and exception restrictions. It must retain refusal for generic unrelated-pthread entry, including an advertised “serialized foreign-thread” mode. Serialized survival is positive experimental evidence, not qualification of that mode.\n\n**C3 — MT experiment and standard of proof**\n\n**The MT blocking verdict is confirmed; the complete five-step qualification is not finished.**\n\n| Experiment requirement | Assessment |\n|---|---|\n| Pinned, genuinely SMP-enabled build | Confirmed by configuration/bootstrap records and live behavior |\n| Explicit processor startup | Confirmed: `-:p2`; server sets parallelism 2 |\n| B executing elsewhere while A blocks; real GC before release | Implemented; failure reproduced |\n| Valid entries, owner control, foreign-entry lifecycle investigation | Partly satisfied; attach stress has defects described below |\n| macOS arm64 and Linux x86_64 | macOS executed; Linux pending |\n\nThe bootstrap distinction is substantive: regenerating the runtime modules with the SMP prelude is documented at `MT/BUILD-CONFIG.md:69–115`. The current generated scheduler contains the cited SMP implementation, and my W0MT rerun reports two processors, TLS pstate access, and `smp-feature=ENABLED`.\n\nI also compared the ten restored source-tree runtime `.c` files’ actual Git object hashes against the pin; all match. This independently checks restoration after the disclosed regeneration incident.\n\n**The decisive blocked-GC result reproduces.**\n\nIn my fresh MT run:\n\n- A announces entry on processor 1.\n- B observes A inside C and requests `(##gc)` on processor 0.\n- C returns after its approximately six-second deadline.\n- B’s first GC-round completion and release handshake occur afterward.\n\nEvidence: `W/build/review-mt/w2mt-blocked.out:7–16,25–29`. The committed record shows the same sequence (`MT/witnesses/results/w2mt-blocked.out:8–17,26–30`).\n\nThe synchronization source supports the mechanism: `___garbage_collect` calls `on_all_processors`; that interrupts participating processors and enters the barrier (`S/lib/setup.c:1138–1149,1182–1196`). Additional Scheme progress does not make the blocked processor participate.\n\n**Foreign-entry evidence supports refusal, with narrower conclusions.**\n\nI reproduced the MT owner control PASS and naive serialized-entry SIGSEGV. Valid signatures are present (`MT/witnesses/w3mt-server.scm:16–23`); the committed concurrent naive run also segfaults (`MT/witnesses/results/w3mt-p2.out:4–6`).\n\nThe exploratory bridge initializes slot 2 while leaving `processor_count=2`, then binds TLS (`MT/witnesses/w3mt-main.c:194–229`). That omission is independently visible in source: VM interrupts cover only counted processors (`S/lib/setup.c:95–106`). TLS binding plus successful arithmetic is not a complete attachment protocol.\n\nTwo qualifications are necessary:\n\n- **New P2 finding — teardown corruption is NOT CONFIRMED.** `keep_attached` is documented and passed as true, but never honored. The helper unconditionally clears TLS and cleans up the pstate (`MT/witnesses/w3mt-main.c:188–192,275–286`). Its caller then invokes Scheme in the purported “while attached” loop (`:325–331`). The recorded crash follows that cleanup (`MT/witnesses/results/w3mt-bridge-concurrent.out:12–15`). This is an invalid post-detachment entry, not an isolated demonstration that teardown corrupted a correctly attached runtime.\n- **P2 evidence limitation — the bridge GC timeouts do not isolate membership as their sole cause.** In the serialized bridge and reattach tests, the owner is parked in `pthread_join` (`MT/witnesses/w3mt-main.c:434–440,470–472`). That counted processor cannot service GC synchronization either. The recorded 120/180-second timeouts establish failure of these attempted sequences; they do not prove that every possible attachment sequence must deadlock. Repeated attachment and later lifecycle checks were never reached.\n\n**New P3 finding — “collection completed 183µs after C’s return” is NOT CONFIRMED.** The 183µs value is a GC-report duration, not a measured return-to-completion interval (`MT/witnesses/results/w2mt-blocked.out:13–17`). GC timing begins inside the collector (`S/lib/mem.c:6954–6961`). The record establishes completion after C returns and an approximately six-second request-to-round-completion wait. It does not establish that precise 183µs lag.\n\nThus, **MT-VERDICT’s operational conclusion is correct**: this tested configuration fails the blocking contract, and foreign-thread entry remains unqualified. Its teardown attribution and universal attachment-impossibility wording need amendment. The startup/heap crashes remain configuration-specific observations, not established root-cause diagnoses.\n\n**C4 — Final track verdict under astra’s criteria**\n\nYes: the standing ruling is **PARTIAL-GO for bounded P1b work, conditional on qualification of each implemented capability; blocking and foreign-thread rows retain honest refusal; P2/P3 remain Chez-only.**\n\nAstra §5 states verbatim:\n\n> **Partial GO:** memory, loading, ordinary calls, and errno may land independently when qualified. Retain blocking/callback refusals and describe the result as partial FFI. This does **not** authorize P2/P3 parity claims.\n\nIts NO-GO criteria are:\n\n> **NO-GO for P2/P3:** collection cannot complete while C is blocked; unrelated C-thread entry cannot be made safe where required; exact runtime ownership rules remain unknown; or the solution depends on silently moving generic calls to another native thread.\n\nThe blocking failure independently sustains NO-GO on both tested configurations. No universal foreign-entry impossibility proof is required to reach that ruling.\n\nThe next-path language remains:\n\n> If 4.9.8 cannot meet the necessary runtime transitions, the next decision is an explicit Gambit upgrade or runtime patch through the upgrade gate. The fallback remains honest refusal, with Chez retaining the affected capability.\n\nP1b must not assert:\n\n- GC-safe blocking or blocking-plus-errno qualification.\n- Generic foreign-pthread callbacks, including serialized entry.\n- Arbitrary dynamic closure-backed callables; F6’s architecture/trampoline constraint remains.\n- Unrestricted yielding, migration, exception escape, or nested callback behavior.\n- Qualified memory, loader, ABI, or public jolt behavior solely from these standalone fixtures.\n- Linux qualification, full FFI parity, or qualification of AOT/FASL/subprocess capabilities.\n\nQualified callable modes can be included only with their own public-path behavioral gates and explicit restrictions.\n\n**C5 — Readiness for a P1b brief**\n\n**No further runtime experiment is necessary before writing the bounded brief.** It should:\n\n1. Pin the exact runtime/configuration; MT is an unsuccessful experiment, not a qualified replacement.\n2. Carry the inventory Q1–Q6, F6 trampoline constraint, real-GC replacement, and per-capability refusals.\n3. Define callable ownership and lifetime rules, catch exceptions inside callbacks, and reject unsupported native-thread entry before executing Scheme.\n4. Require target-identified public jolt tests, both platform legs for acceptance, negative controls, patch/seed reproducibility, rollback, and preserved regressions (`Plan:267–275`).\n5. Correct or explicitly exclude the MT teardown and timing claims above.\n\nLinux and remaining lifecycle experiments are prerequisites for future qualification claims, not blockers to documenting and implementing the bounded slice.\n\nCONFIRMED-VERDICT — PARTIAL-GO for bounded P1b memory/loader/ordinary-call/errno work and independently qualified callable modes; blocking and foreign-thread rows remain refused, with P2/P3 Chez-only unless a separately chosen upgrade/runtime-patch path passes qualification.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
