{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat host/chez/remint.sh; sed -n '290,348p' host/gambit/eval-test.ss; sed -n '455,510p' jolt-core/jolt/backend_scheme/ffi.clj; sed -n '685,720p' host/gambit/scheme-adapter-runtime.ss; cat host/gambit/ffi/joltffi-fixture.c; sed -n '1,85p' host/gambit/gambitcheck.ss; rg -n 'define.*sa-os-family|define.*sa-stats|weak|will' host/gambit/{prelude-shims,scheme-adapter-runtime}.ss; rg -n '\"'^CHEZ'\"' Makefile; sed -n '100,137p' /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/jolt/1795020000000-engineer-p1b-inventory.glm-5.3-flash.findings.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat host/chez/remint.sh; sed -n '290,348p' host/gambit/eval-test.ss; sed -n '455,510p' jolt-core/jolt/backend_scheme/ffi.clj; sed -n '685,720p' host/gambit/scheme-adapter-runtime.ss; cat host/gambit/ffi/joltffi-fixture.c; sed -n '1,85p' host/gambit/gambitcheck.ss; rg -n 'define.*sa-os-family|define.*sa-stats|weak|will' host/gambit/{prelude-shims,scheme-adapter-runtime}.ss; rg -n '\"'^CHEZ'\"' Makefile; sed -n '100,137p' /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/jolt/1795020000000-engineer-p1b-inventory.glm-5.3-flash.findings.md\"","aggregated_output":"#!/bin/sh\n# Re-mint the checked-in Chez seed. Run after changing a seed source — the reader\n# (host/chez/reader.ss), the analyzer/IR/backend (jolt-core/jolt/*.clj), or the\n# clojure.core overlay (jolt-core/clojure/core/*.clj). Iterates bootstrap.ss from the\n# current seed to a byte-fixpoint and overwrites host/chez/seed/, then cross-mints\n# host/gambit/seed/ from it.\nset -e\nroot=\"$(CDPATH= cd -- \"$(dirname -- \"$0\")/../..\" && pwd)\"\ncd \"$root\"\n# JOLT_CHEZ wins (see host/chez/selfcheck.sh) — re-minting the seed under the\n# wrong Chez is exactly the mistake this script exists to avoid.\nCHEZ=\"${JOLT_CHEZ:-${CHEZ:-$(command -v chez || command -v chezscheme || command -v scheme)}}\"\ntmp=\"$(mktemp -d)\"\ntrap 'rm -rf \"$tmp\"' EXIT\n\ncp host/chez/seed/prelude.ss \"$tmp/cur-p.ss\"\ncp host/chez/seed/image.ss   \"$tmp/cur-i.ss\"\ni=0\nwhile [ \"$i\" -lt 8 ]; do\n  i=$((i + 1))\n  # capture stderr so the fixpoint pass can be checked for skipped forms; the\n  # skip count is only trustworthy once converged (an earlier pass compiling off\n  # an older seed may skip a form that a later pass, off the rebuilt seed, emits).\n  # a bootstrap failure must leave its error readable: the temp dir dies with\n  # the trap, so surface stderr before set -e aborts the loop\n  # JOLT_MINT_DEBUG puts each skipped form's reason in the log. A form a pass\n  # skips is missing from the seed the next pass runs, so when that form is part\n  # of the compiler, the pass that fails is a later one and its own error (an\n  # unbound jv$ name, say) points nowhere near the cause: show what the passes\n  # before it skipped, and why.\n  if ! JOLT_MINT_DEBUG=1 \"$CHEZ\" --script host/chez/bootstrap.ss \\\n      \"$tmp/cur-p.ss\" \"$tmp/cur-i.ss\" \"$tmp/new-p.ss\" \"$tmp/new-i.ss\" >/dev/null 2>\"$tmp/err\"; then\n    echo \"re-mint: bootstrap pass $i failed:\" >&2\n    grep -v '^mint: ' \"$tmp/err\" >&2 || true\n    if [ -s \"$tmp/skips\" ]; then\n      echo \"re-mint: forms the earlier passes skipped:\" >&2\n      cat \"$tmp/skips\" >&2\n    fi\n    exit 1\n  fi\n  grep '^mint: .* raised: ' \"$tmp/err\" | sed \"s/^/pass $i: /\" >>\"$tmp/skips\" || true\n  if diff -q \"$tmp/cur-p.ss\" \"$tmp/new-p.ss\" >/dev/null \\\n     && diff -q \"$tmp/cur-i.ss\" \"$tmp/new-i.ss\" >/dev/null; then\n    skipped=$(sed -n 's/^mint: \\([0-9][0-9]*\\) form(s) skipped$/\\1/p' \"$tmp/err\" | tail -1)\n    if [ -n \"$skipped\" ] && [ \"$skipped\" -ne 0 ]; then\n      echo \"re-mint: $skipped form(s) failed to compile in the fixpoint pass:\" >&2\n      grep '^mint: .* raised: ' \"$tmp/err\" >&2\n      exit 1\n    fi\n    cp \"$tmp/new-p.ss\" host/chez/seed/prelude.ss\n    cp \"$tmp/new-i.ss\" host/chez/seed/image.ss\n    echo \"re-minted seed (converged after $i pass(es))\"\n    # The gambit seed is cross-minted FROM this one (host/gambit/gen-seed.ss\n    # re-emits it with the :gambit target), and gambitseedcheck in CI holds the\n    # two to the same sources. Re-minting only the chez half left it stale, which\n    # is how a contributor who did exactly what CONTRIBUTING said arrived with a\n    # red gate (#1057). set -e: a gen-seed failure fails the remint.\n    \"$CHEZ\" --script host/gambit/gen-seed.ss\n    exit 0\n  fi\n  cp \"$tmp/new-p.ss\" \"$tmp/cur-p.ss\"\n  cp \"$tmp/new-i.ss\" \"$tmp/cur-i.ss\"\ndone\necho \"re-mint did not converge in 8 passes\" >&2\nexit 1\n(check \"(let [b (fn [^double d] (byte d)) c (fn [^double d] (char d)) m (fn [t] (try (t) (catch IllegalArgumentException e (ex-message e))))] [(m (fn [] (b ##NaN))) (m (fn [] (b 127.9))) (m (fn [] (c ##NaN))) (byte (identity ##NaN)) (byte (identity 127.9)) (m (fn [] (byte (identity ##Inf)))) (m (fn [] (byte (float ##NaN))))])\"\n       \"[\\\"Value out of range for byte: NaN\\\" \\\"Value out of range for byte: 127.9\\\" \\\"Value out of range for char: NaN\\\" 0 127 \\\"Value out of range for long: Infinity\\\" \\\"Value out of range for byte: NaN\\\"]\")\n\n;; a def that shadows a clojure.core name compiles, and warns on stderr the way\n;; the JVM does. The warning goes through *err*, whose default is a port-writer\n;; jhost with no host methods on this target: dispatching write on it threw, so\n;; every such def failed to compile here (ns.ss ns-err-println).\n(let* ((ep (open-output-string))\n       (v (parameterize ((current-error-port ep))\n            (guard (e (#t \"THREW\"))\n              (jolt-pr-readable (jolt-compile-eval \"(defn vals [] 1)\" \"user\")))))\n       (err (get-output-string ep))\n       (want \"WARNING: vals already refers to: #'clojure.core/vals in namespace: user, being replaced by: #'user/vals\\n\"))\n  (if (and (string=? v \"#'user/vals\") (string=? err want))\n      (printf \"  ok     (defn vals [] 1) => ~a, warned on stderr\\n\" v)\n      (begin (printf \"  FAIL   (defn vals [] 1): got ~s with stderr ~s\\n\" v err)\n             (set! failures (+ failures 1)))))\n(check \"(vals)\" \"1\")\n\n;; jolt.ffi/pointer-fn has no gambit lowering (no ffi tier here): the binding\n;; defines, scalar or by-value, and calling it raises an exception jolt can\n;; catch, naming the form and the target. Not a Chez capture: on Chez the same\n;; call through a NULL pointer raises NullPointerException instead.\n(check \"(def pf (jolt.ffi/__cfn-pointer [:int64] :int64))\" \"#'user/pf\")\n(check \"(def pb (jolt.ffi/__cfn-pointer [[:by-value [:struct [[:a :int]]]]] :int64))\" \"#'user/pb\")\n(check \"(try (pf 1 2) (catch Throwable t (ex-message t)))\"\n       \"\\\"jolt.ffi/pointer-fn: ffi is unsupported on the gambit target\\\"\")\n(check \"(try (pb 1 2) (catch UnsupportedOperationException t (ex-message t)))\"\n       \"\\\"jolt.ffi/pointer-fn: ffi is unsupported on the gambit target\\\"\")\n\n;; A by-value jolt.ffi callback has no gambit lowering (no ffi tier here, and no\n;; define-ftype): making one raises an exception jolt can catch, naming the form\n;; and the target, for a struct argument and a struct result alike. Not a Chez\n;; capture: on Chez the same forms build a callback.\n(check \"(try (jolt.ffi/__ccallable (fn [p] 0) [[:by-value [:struct [[:a :int]]]]] :int) (catch UnsupportedOperationException t (ex-message t)))\"\n       \"\\\"jolt.ffi/foreign-callable: ffi is unsupported on the gambit target\\\"\")\n(check \"(try (jolt.ffi/__ccallable (fn [d] nil) [:int] [:by-value [:struct [[:a :int]]]]) (catch Throwable t (ex-message t)))\"\n       \"\\\"jolt.ffi/foreign-callable: ffi is unsupported on the gambit target\\\"\")\n\n(printf \"eval-test: ~a failure(s)\\n\" failures)\n(if (= failures 0)\n    (begin (display \"eval-test: PASS — jolt compiles and evaluates on native gsi\\n\") (exit 0))\n    (exit 1))\n;; Resolution order is the same for every binding, variadic or not: a declared\n;; :jolt/native's own dlopen handle first, the process-global table second. A\n;; scalar-varargs binding used to skip the scoped branch, which meant a variadic\n;; symbol in a library loaded with load-library could not be bound AT ALL --\n;; RTLD_LOCAL keeps it out of the global table, so the name found nothing and the\n;; call raised \"no entry\". curl_easy_setopt is that case. The address+convention\n;; form this emits is the one the aggregate C witness already covers on each\n;; target ABI.\n(defn- ffi-named-procedure-expr [node vi signature]\n  (let [csym (chez-str-lit (:csym node))\n        capture (:capture-native-error node)\n        blocking (:blocking node)\n        conv (if vi (str \" (__varargs_after \" vi \")\") \"\")\n        capture-conv (cond\n                       blocking \"__collect_safe\"\n                       ;; The outer wrapper already adds one list. A compound\n                       ;; convention needs one more so the adapter receives\n                       ;; (__varargs_after n) as one datum rather than two\n                       ;; flat convention arguments.\n                       vi (str \"(__varargs_after \" vi \")\")\n                       :else \"\")\n        by-name (if capture\n                  (str \"(jolt-ffi-native-error-procedure (\" capture-conv \") \"\n                       csym signature \")\")\n                  (str \"(\" (if blocking\n                              \"sa-foreign-procedure-blocking \"\n                              \"sa-foreign-procedure \")\n                       conv \" \" csym signature \")\"))\n        ;; P1b-stage1 gap G-3 — astra: replace the raw Chez scoped-address\n        ;; emission with adapter operations. On :gambit the scoped branch\n        ;; routes through the adapter syntaxes too: the adapter shape\n        ;; registry raises deliberately for unregistered shapes, never leaving\n        ;; the call to an unbound Chez foreign-procedure identifier.\n        scoped (if (= :gambit (target))\n                  (str \"(let ((a (jolt-ffi-dlsym-native \" csym \"))) \"\n                       \"(and a \"\n                       (if capture\n                         (str \"(jolt-ffi-native-error-procedure (\" capture-conv \") a\"\n                              signature \")\")\n                         (str \"(sa-foreign-procedure a\" signature \")\"))\n                       \"))\")\n                  (str \"(let ((a (jolt-ffi-dlsym-native \" csym \"))) \"\n                       \"(and a \"\n                       (if capture\n                         (str \"(jolt-ffi-native-error-procedure (\" capture-conv \") a\"\n                              signature \")\")\n                         (str \"(foreign-procedure\"\n                              (when blocking \" __collect_safe\")\n                              (when vi conv)\n                              \" a\" signature \")\"))\n                       \"))\"))]\n    (str \"(or p (begin (set! p (or \" scoped \" \" by-name \")) p))\")))\n\n;; jolt.ffi/pointer-fn: the procedure is built against the address in the\n;; binding's first argument, on every call. A pointer-held callback can differ\n;; from one call to the next (each GhosttyClipboardRead carries its own reply),\n;; (sa-foreign-procedure-runtime name args res blocking?) -> raise\n;; REFUSED: constructing procedures for RUNTIME-discovered signatures is the\n;; open-ended shape space a finite registry cannot claim (astra §2; the\n;; inventory's :runtime-signature-eval class).\n(define (sa-foreign-procedure-runtime name args res blocking?)\n  (error 'sa-foreign-procedure-runtime\n         \"runtime-signature ffi construction is unsupported on the gambit target — a finite shim registry cannot claim runtime-discovered signatures\"))\n\n;; ---- memory ops (qualified; through the bridge) -------------------------------\n;; Pointers are exact integers (jolt's own pointer semantics — a jolt pointer\n;; IS a raw address). Widths are exact for both supported legs (LP64).\n\n(define (sa-foreign-alloc n)\n  (joltffi-ensure-bridge! 'sa-foreign-alloc)\n  (if (exact-integer? n)\n      (joltffi-malloc n)\n      (error 'sa-foreign-alloc \"jolt.ffi: allocation size must be an exact integer\" n)))\n\n(define (sa-foreign-free p)\n  (joltffi-ensure-bridge! 'sa-foreign-free)\n  (joltffi-free p))\n\n;; typed access: the TYPE VOCABULARY is the contract's — Chez's foreign type\n;; symbols (the values jolt.ffi's ffi-type->chez produces). Reads/writes go\n;; through memcpy-based bridge helpers, so unaligned offsets behave.\n(define (sa-foreign-ref type addr off)\n  (joltffi-ensure-bridge! 'sa-foreign-ref)\n  (case type\n    ((int) (joltffi-ref-i32 addr off))\n    ((unsigned-int) (joltffi-ref-u32 addr off))\n    ((integer-8) (joltffi-ref-i8 addr off))\n    ((unsigned-8) (joltffi-ref-u8 addr off))\n    ((integer-16) (joltffi-ref-i16 addr off))\n    ((unsigned-16) (joltffi-ref-u16 addr off))\n    ((integer-32) (joltffi-ref-i32 addr off))\n    ((unsigned-32) (joltffi-ref-u32 addr off))\n/* joltffi-fixture.c — the stage-1 gate fixture library (P1b-stage1).\n *\n * One extern \"C\" target per registered ordinary ABI shape the bridge carries\n * (p1b-inv/shim-registry.edn), plus the errno negative controls the repaired\n * W1-r2 pattern requires, and the errno-value probes so the assertions hold on\n * any OS. Built by the gambitffi* Makefile targets into\n * build/gambit-ffi/libjoltffi-fixture.dylib (and the two loader-smoke\n * libraries) with cc, loaded through jolt's own loader path.\n *\n * The TARGETS own their errno, exactly as W1's repair demands:\n *   - capture targets set errno themselves and return -1 (failure), or return\n *     a success value and PRESERVE the caller's errno (contract: do not\n *     normalize success to zero);\n *   - the intervening/post-capture clobbers are written AFTER the \"target\"\n *     portion of the call returns, so a shim that captures immediately defeats\n *     them, and one that does not is observable.\n *\n * Every symbol is exported with default visibility so dlsym sees it.\n */\n\n#include <errno.h>\n#include <stdarg.h>\n#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n#include <string.h>\n\n#define JOLTFFI_CLOBBER 7777\n\n/* ---- errno probes (s01-shaped, so the gate reads them through the tier) ---- */\nint joltffi_fx_eagain (void) { return EAGAIN; }\nint joltffi_fx_eacces (void) { return EACCES; }\nint joltffi_fx_ebadf  (void) { return EBADF; }\nint joltffi_fx_eio    (void) { return EIO; }\nint joltffi_fx_eintr_c (void) { return EINTR; }\nint joltffi_fx_clobber_const (void) { return JOLTFFI_CLOBBER; }\n\n/* ---- ordinary shapes ------------------------------------------------------- */\n\n/* s01: () -> int */\nint joltffi_fx_layout_version (void) { return 42; }\n\n/* s01b: () -> int32 */\nint32_t joltffi_fx_eintr (void) { return (int32_t) EINTR; }\n\n/* s02: (void*, size_t) -> int — provenance-style writer */\nint joltffi_fx_provenance (void *buf, size_t n)\n{\n  const char *text = \"joltffi-fixture\";\n  size_t len = strlen (text);\n  if (buf == NULL || n < len + 1)\n    return -1;\n  memcpy (buf, text, len + 1);\n  return (int) len;\n}\n\n/* s03: (void*, size_t) -> void */\nvoid joltffi_fx_cleanse (void *p, size_t n) { memset (p, 0, n); }\n\n/* s03-shaped errno clobber: sets errno AFTER zeroing, so a capture call made\n   after it reads the clobber and an EARLIER captured pair proves untouched. */\nvoid joltffi_fx_seterrno_cleanse (void *p, size_t n)\n{\n  if (n > 0)\n    memset (p, 0, n);\n  errno = JOLTFFI_CLOBBER;\n}\n\n/* s12: (void*, int64) -> int32 — big-endian field read */\nint32_t joltffi_fx_s32be_get (void *p, int64_t off)\n{\n  const unsigned char *b = (const unsigned char *) p + off;\n  return (int32_t) (((uint32_t) b[0] << 24) | ((uint32_t) b[1] << 16)\n                    | ((uint32_t) b[2] << 8) | (uint32_t) b[3]);\n}\n\n/* s12b: (void*, int64, int32) -> void — big-endian field write */\nvoid joltffi_fx_s32be_put (void *p, int64_t off, int32_t v)\n{\n  unsigned char *b = (unsigned char *) p + off;\n  uint32_t u = (uint32_t) v;\n  b[0] = (unsigned char) (u >> 24);\n  b[1] = (unsigned char) (u >> 16);\n  b[2] = (unsigned char) (u >> 8);\n  b[3] = (unsigned char) u;\n}\n\n/* s13: (int64, void*, int64) -> int64 — decimal error-text writer */\nint64_t joltffi_fx_strerror (int64_t code, void *buf, int64_t n)\n{\n  if (buf == NULL || n < 2)\n    return -1;\n  int written = snprintf ((char *) buf, (size_t) n, \"err-%lld\", (long long) code);\n  return (int64_t) written;\n}\n\n/* s18: (void*) -> void */\nvoid joltffi_fx_free (void *p) { free (p); }\n\n/* s19: () -> void* */\nvoid *joltffi_fx_worker_new (void) { return malloc (64); }\n\n/* s20: () -> void* — the errno ACCESSOR shape (the ffi/errno read path) */\nstatic int joltffi_fx_errno_cell;\nvoid *joltffi_fx_errno_location (void) { return &joltffi_fx_errno_cell; }\n\n/* s21: (int) -> string — nullable char* return (NULL -> jolt nil) */\nstatic const char *joltffi_fx_errtext_one = \"joltffi-error-one\";\nstatic const char *joltffi_fx_errtext_two = \"joltffi-error-two\";\nconst char *joltffi_fx_errtext (int code)\n{\n  switch (code)\n    {\n    case 1: return joltffi_fx_errtext_one;\n    case 2: return joltffi_fx_errtext_two;\n    default: return NULL;             /* NULL must surface as jolt nil */\n    }\n}\n\n/* ---- declared-tail varargs shapes (variadic boundary) ---------------------- */\n\n/* s22 / s22b: (int, int, ...) -> int. The declared tail is ONE int. cmd==99\n   sets EAGAIN and returns -1 (capture targets own their errno); otherwise the\n   sum travels back with the caller's errno untouched. */\nint joltffi_fx_vfcntl (int fd, int cmd, ...)\n{\n  va_list ap;\n  int tail;\n  va_start (ap, cmd);\n  tail = va_arg (ap, int);\n  va_end (ap);\n  if (cmd == 99)\n    {\n      errno = EAGAIN;\n      return -1;\n    }\n  return fd + cmd + tail;\n}\n\n/* s22c: (int, unsigned long, ...) -> int with capture. req==88 sets EBADF. */\nint joltffi_fx_vioctl (int fd, unsigned long req, ...)\n{\n  va_list ap;\n  void *arg;\n  va_start (ap, req);\n  arg = va_arg (ap, void *);\n  va_end (ap);\n  if (req == 88)\n    {\n      errno = EBADF;\n      return -1;\n    }\n  return fd + (int) (req & 0xff) + (arg != NULL ? 1 : 0);\n}\n\n/* ---- capture shapes (ordinary, no blocking): errno is the target's own ----- */\n\n/* s23: (int, int, int, void*, void*) -> int */\nint joltffi_fx_getsockoptlike (int fd, int lvl, int opt, void *val, void *len)\n{\n  if (fd < 0)\n    {\n      errno = EBADF;\n      return -1;\n    }\n  return fd + lvl + opt + (val != NULL ? 1 : 0) + (len != NULL ? 1 : 0);\n}\n\n/* s26: (int, int, int) -> int */\nint joltffi_fx_socketlike (int a, int b, int c)\n{\n  if (a < 0)\n    {\n      errno = EACCES;\n      return -1;\n    }\n  return a + b + c;\n}\n\n/* s26b: (int, void*, int) -> int */\nint joltffi_fx_bindlike (int fd, void *addr, int len)\n{\n  if (fd < 0)\n    {\n      errno = EACCES;\n      return -1;\n    }\n  return fd + len + (addr != NULL ? 1 : 0);\n}\n\n/* s26c: (int, int) -> int */\nint joltffi_fx_listenlike (int fd, int backlog)\n{\n  if (fd < 0)\n    {\n      errno = EBADF;\n      return -1;\n    }\n  return fd * 10 + backlog;\n}\n\n/* s26d: (int, int, int, void*, int) -> int */\nint joltffi_fx_setsockoptlike (int fd, int lvl, int opt, void *val, int len)\n{\n  if (fd < 0)\n    {\n      errno = EBADF;\n      return -1;\n    }\n  return fd + lvl + opt + len + (val != NULL ? 1 : 0);\n}\n\n/* s26e: (int, void*, void*) -> int */\nint joltffi_fx_getsocknamelike (int fd, void *addr, void *len)\n{\n  if (fd < 0)\n    {\n      errno = EBADF;\n      return -1;\n    }\n  return fd + (addr != NULL ? 2 : 0) + (len != NULL ? 3 : 0);\n}\n\n/* ---- loader smoke helpers ---------------------------------------------------\n   Exported twice (here and in the fixture-2 duplicate library) so the gate can\n   pin the duplicate-definers detection: two ADDRESSES, first declared wins. */\nint joltffi_fx_dup_symbol (void) { return 111; }\n\n/* Present-but-unloadable: the Makefile links a copy of this library against a\n   dependency dylib it then REMOVES from the build dir, so dlopen fails with the\n   loader's own preserved reason (a missing dependency, not a missing file). */\nint joltffi_fx_never_called (void) { return -2; }\n;; gambitcheck.ss — the G1 gate for the Gambit adapter (jolt-mj95.2).\n;;\n;; Run via `make gambitcheck` (detection-gated: skips when gambit-scheme is\n;; absent, and always uses $(brew --prefix gambit-scheme)/bin/gsi — NEVER bare\n;; gsc/gsi, which is Ghostscript on this machine). Run from the repo root.\n;;\n;; Loads the three gambit target files (prelude-shims, scheme-adapter-runtime,\n;; hasheq), then:\n;;   (a) asserts every host/scheme-adapter/CONTRACT.txt name is bound\n;;       (or shimmed — variables via eval, syntax shims via the explicit list,\n;;       each ALSO verified behaviorally by the unit tests below),\n;;   (b) unit-tests every shim group: record round-trip incl. the parent\n;;       clause family, hashtable CRUD + entries + weak-eq, fx + bitwise\n;;       aliases, condition accessors on a caught error, condvar wait/signal,\n;;       parameter fork-inheritance, with-mutex, hasheq known-answer rows\n;;       (captured from the Chez build via bin/jolt), and the sa-* raise\n;;       surface (message-carrying conditions).\n;;\n;; The hasheq known-answer rows pin VALUE PARITY with the Chez build: each row\n;; is a pure hasheq function whose output was captured from `bin/jolt` on the\n;; Chez runtime (the jolt-level (hash ...) routes through exactly these\n;; functions). Rows for the vector/map shapes compute the hash-ordered /\n;; hash-unordered combinator math directly — the cseq/pmap iteration over them\n;; is G2 scope (the full runtime), the murmur3 + combinator math is G1.\n;;\n;; NO import here: the prelude's (import (except (gambit) define-record-type))\n;; is the single import point for the whole boot path. A file-level import in\n;; THIS file would create a module boundary and the loaded shim macros\n;; (define-record-type, fx aliases, with-mutex, the sa-foreign-* syntaxes)\n;; would not resolve here — the (gambit) names come from the prelude's import,\n;; which loads into the same top level.\n\n;; ---- splice the three target files ------------------------------------------\n;;\n;; ##include (NOT load): each loaded file is a SEPARATE compilation unit, and a\n;; define-syntax inside a loaded file registers only in its own unit's macro\n;; environment — the loading file's LATER forms never see it (defines leak at\n;; runtime into the shared global, macros don't). The prelude's record/fx/\n;; with-mutex shims are macros, so gambitcheck must expand in the SAME unit as\n;; the prelude: ##include textually splices the three files here, exactly the\n;; \"compile the boot files together\" flow the G2 build uses.\n\n(##include \"prelude-shims.ss\")\n(##include \"scheme-adapter-runtime.ss\")\n\n;; hasheq's seq/symbol arms test jolt-nil? — a values.ss kernel name that only\n;; boots in G2. A sentinel stub stands in for this gate; the known-answer rows\n;; below never construct jolt-nil, so the stub is only ever asked \"is this\n;; ordinary value nil\" (answer: no). G2's boot manifest loads the real one.\n(define %gambitcheck-nil-sentinel (list 'nil))\n(define (jolt-nil? x) (eq? x %gambitcheck-nil-sentinel))\n\n(##include \"hasheq.ss\")\n\n;; ---- test harness ------------------------------------------------------------\n\n(define failures 0)\n\n(define (check label actual expected)\n  (if (equal? actual expected)\n      (begin (printf \"  ok     ~a\\n\" label) #t)\n      (begin (printf \"  FAIL   ~a: got ~s expected ~s\\n\" label actual expected)\n             (set! failures (+ failures 1))\n             #f)))\n\n(define (check-true label v)\n  (if v\n      (begin (printf \"  ok     ~a\\n\" label) #t)\n      (begin (printf \"  FAIL   ~a: got #f\\n\" label)\n             (set! failures (+ failures 1))\n             #f)))\n\n(define (check-raise-message label thunk expected-msg)\n  (let ((m (guard (e (#t (condition-message e)))\n             (thunk)\n             \"NO-RAISE\")))\n    (check (string-append label \" -> \" expected-msg)\n           m expected-msg)))\n\n;; ---- (a) contract-name assertion pass ----------------------------------------\n\n;; minimal line parsing, self-contained (mirrors host/scheme-adapter/chez.ss)\n(define (char-ws? c)\n  (or (char=? c #\\space) (char=? c #\\tab) (char=? c #\\newline)\n      (char=? c #\\return)))\nhost/gambit/scheme-adapter-runtime.ss:59:;; System.gc callers mean: weak references clear and guardians fire.\nhost/gambit/scheme-adapter-runtime.ss:85:;; references before the call observes weak references cleared and the heap\nhost/gambit/scheme-adapter-runtime.ss:261:(define (sa-os-family)\nhost/gambit/scheme-adapter-runtime.ss:291:(define (sa-stats)\nhost/gambit/prelude-shims.ss:264:;; hashtable-set! 97, hashtable-keys 22, hashtable-delete! 17, make-weak-eq-\nhost/gambit/prelude-shims.ss:282:(define (make-weak-eq-hashtable) (make-table test: eq? weak-keys: #t))\nhost/gambit/prelude-shims.ss:283:;; Chez's ephemeron table: a key-weak table whose values may hold their key.\nhost/gambit/prelude-shims.ss:284:;; Gambit's weak-keys table is the closest it has; a value that refers back to\nhost/gambit/prelude-shims.ss:286:(define (make-ephemeron-eq-hashtable) (make-table test: eq? weak-keys: #t))\nhost/gambit/prelude-shims.ss:321:;; test and weakness — records.ss copies the weak deftype-ctor-tag table on\nhost/gambit/prelude-shims.ss:322:;; every deftype and expects the copy to stay weak.\nhost/gambit/prelude-shims.ss:576:;; native thread ids, so a weak table maps thread objects to a monotonic\nhost/gambit/prelude-shims.ss:578:(define thread-id-table (make-table test: eq? weak-keys: #t))\nhost/gambit/prelude-shims.ss:853:(define %jolt-lock-depth (make-table test: eq? weak-keys: #t))\n52:CHEZSCHEME-CC := cc\n56:CHEZSCHEME-CC := $(GCC)\n59:CHEZSCHEME-CC := $(GCC)\n60:CHEZSCHEME-CC-DEP := $(GCC)\n62:CHEZSCHEME-VERSION ?= $(or $(shell sed -n 's/^CHEZSCHEME-VERSION ?= *//p' $M/chezscheme.mk | head -n1),10.4.1)\n63:CHEZSCHEME-LOCAL := $(LOCAL-ROOT)/chezscheme-$(CHEZSCHEME-VERSION)\n64:CHEZSCHEME := $(CHEZSCHEME-LOCAL)/bin/scheme\n66:CHEZSCHEME-STAMP := $(CHEZSCHEME-LOCAL)/jolt-patches-$(shell sh host/chez/provision-chez.sh --hash)\n97:CHEZ ?= $(JOLT-CHEZ)\n   A Gambit port must refuse or re-probe under Gambit's ABI; its exported\n   jolt.ffi surface (stdlib/jolt/ffi.clj:63-75) documents the semantics.\n\n## Open questions for the P1b designer\n\n- **Q1 (gap G-1):** `emit-ffi-node` guards `:gambit` only for pointer-call fns\n  and by-value callables. A `:ffi-layout` on the gambit target emits\n  `define-ftype`/`make-ftype-pointer` (unbound there) and raises\n  unbound-variable at namespace load instead of the documented message.\n  Refuse in the compiler, or commit to a Gambit layout engine?\n- **Q2 (gap G-3):** named scalar/aggregate bindings on `:gambit` emit the same\n  text as Chez; the scoped branch references `jolt-ffi-dlsym-native` and bare\n  `foreign-procedure`, both unbound on the gambit boot — first call dies on an\n  unbound identifier, violating astra's \"raise deliberately, never reach an\n  unbound Chez identifier\". Needs a `:gambit` arm in\n  `ffi-named-procedure-expr` (or an adapter-bound raising `foreign-procedure`).\n- **Q3:** `jolt_parser_worker_free` — now pinned with evidence: the C entry\n  spawns a parse thread and `pthread_join`s it (native/jolt-parser/jolt_parser.cpp:1176-1186,\n  on_parse_thread 769-780) while the binding is not `:blocking`\n  (src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt:66). Qualify the unmarked wait or flip the\n  binding; the registry shape (:shape/s18-ptr-free-ordinary) carries\n  `:open-question :worker-free-blocking-mark`.\n- **Q4:** the `:gambit` target-prims entry (`{:unsafe-prefix \"\"}`,\n  session.clj:76-78) astra asked for still does not exist; emission degrades\n  via `(str nil)` by accident. P1 work, but the inventory confirms the miss.\n- **Q5:** should `sa-foreign-callable`/`-collect-safe` be added to\n  CONTRACT.txt's capability-ffi tier (Surprise 1) before the Gambit adapter\n  work starts, so the contract-name gate holds the boundary?\n- **Q6:** mvn_http's SSL bindings call blocking-capable C (SSL_connect,\n  SSL_read) with no `:blocking` mark — a pre-existing Chez-only assumption.\n  Does the Gambit lane inherit it, refuse libssl surfaces, or re-mark?\n\n## Method note\n\nEvery claim carries file:line read on this SHA. The only executions were\n`git`, `grep`/`sed`, and one `babashka` EDN-read of the registry artifact to\nconfirm it parses (no jolt build, no jolt test, no behavior change). The\nconsumers' C sources and `src/jolt/dao`/`src/jolt/yang` bindings were read\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
