MMEDIATELY after the target returns,\n;;     *errout = saved;                before conversion, cleanup, allocation\n;;     return result;                  or runtime re-entry.\n;;\n;; The errno travels out through a CALLER-ALLOCATED cell (one malloc per call,\n;; allocated by the adapter BEFORE the target call and freed after the Scheme\n;; side reads it) — an invocation-local channel, never a global or TLS \"last\n;; errno\" cell, both of which astra forbids and both of which fail nested and\n;; concurrent calls. Post-capture clobbering (by the target, a later foreign\n;; call, or conversion) cannot reach the captured pair: the W1-r2 negative\n;; controls in joltffi-fixture.c prove the capture position is load-bearing.\n;;\n;; POINTER CONVENTION: pointers cross as exact integers (jolt's own semantics —\n;; a jolt pointer IS a raw address), through the c-lambda `unsigned-long` type\n;; (8 bytes on both supported legs: arm64 macOS, x86_64/arm64 Linux — LP64).\n;; This keeps Gambit pointer-object conversion out of the hot path and makes\n;; address arithmetic behave exactly as Chez's integer pointers do.\n;;\n;; BYTEVECTOR CONVENTION: bulk moves borrow the bytevector's body through\n;; ___BODY_AS(v, ___tU8VECTOR) for the duration of ONE synchronous foreign\n;; call. No collection can run during that call on the provisioned single-\n;; processor runtime (a GC request waits for the blocked processor — codex Q3),\n;; so the borrowed address is stable for the call's duration, exactly the\n;; borrowing argument the Chez adapter makes for its own memcpy moves (which are\n;; deliberately NOT __collect_safe). The bridge never RETAINS a borrowed body.\n;;\n;; errno ATOMICITY at the boundary relies on nothing outside the shim body:\n;; the c-lambda call machinery does not read or write errno (verified: it saves\n;; and restores only Scheme registers — gambit.h FFI macros, codex F2 audit).\n\n(c-declare #<<JOLTFFI-C-BEGIN\n\n#include <errno.h>\n#include <stdint.h>\n#include <st